  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The velocity of a particle is $(-3i+5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2i-j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v=($\n{{field-1}}$i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$j\\,)\\,\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=-3i+5j,\\,a=2i-j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(-3i+5j)+(2i-j)(4) \\\\  & =(-3i+5j)+(8i-4j) \\\\  & =(5i+1j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
